{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This month is for you the beginning of months</b> — This is the first commandment that HaShem commanded Israel through Moses. Therefore Scripture says \"in the land of Egypt,\" because the rest of the commandments in the Torah were given at Mount Sinai. Or perhaps \"in the land of Egypt\" comes to say: not in the city of Egypt, as our Sages stated (Mekhilta, here): outside the metropolis.\n\nNow it would have been fitting for Scripture to say first, \"Speak to all the congregation of Israel, saying: This month is for you,\" and complete the passage. But Moses and Aaron stand in the place of Israel, and He said <b>for you</b> with reference to Israel for their generations, and then returned and said, \"Speak to all the congregation of Israel,\" to command them a one-time commandment regarding the taking of the Egyptian Passover, from the tenth day of the month.\n\nAccording to the Midrash, <b>for you</b> comes to teach that sanctification of the new month requires a court of experts (Rosh HaShanah 25a), and therefore Scripture did not initially say, \"Speak to all the congregation of Israel,\" for the sanctification of the new month involves only Moses and Aaron and their like. The meaning of <b>this month is for you the beginning of months</b> is that Israel shall count it as the first month, and from it they shall count all the months — second and third — until the completion of the year in twelve months, so that this will be a remembrance of the great miracle. For whenever we mention the months, the miracle will be recalled. And therefore the months have no names in the Torah; rather, Scripture says \"in the third month\" (Exodus 19:1), and says \"And it came to pass in the second year, in the second month, the cloud was taken up\" (Numbers 10:11), \"and in the seventh month, on the first day of the month\" etc. (Numbers 29:1), and so all of them.\n\nJust as the remembrance of the Sabbath day comes through our counting from it — one for the Sabbath, and second for the Sabbath — as I shall explain (Ramban on Exodus 20:8), so too the remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt comes through our counting the first month and the second month and the third month for our redemption. For this counting is not for the year, since the beginning of our years is from Tishrei, as it is written (Exodus 34:22), \"and the feast of ingathering at the turn of the year,\" and it is written (Exodus 23:16), \"at the going out of the year.\" If so, when Nisan is called first and Tishrei is called seventh, the explanation is: the first is for redemption, and the seventh is toward it. And this is the meaning of <b>it is first for you</b> — it is not first in the year, but it is first for the name that is called upon it, as a remembrance of our redemption.\n\nOur Sages already mentioned this matter, and they said: the names of the months came up with us from Babylonia (Jerusalem Rosh HaShanah 1:2; Bereshit Rabbah 48:9), for at first they had no names among us. The reason for this is that at first their counting was a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt, but when we came up from Babylonia and that which Scripture said was fulfilled (Jeremiah 16:14–15), \"'As HaShem lives, who brought up the children of Israel from the land of Egypt' shall no longer be said, but rather 'As HaShem lives, who brought up and who brought the children of Israel from the land of the north'\" — we reverted to calling the months by the names they were called in the land of Babylonia, to recall that there we stood and from there HaShem brought us up. For these names — Nisan, Iyar, and the rest — are Persian names, and they are found only in the books of the prophets of Babylonia (Zechariah 1:7; Ezra 6:15; Nehemiah 1:1) and in the Scroll of Esther (3:7). Therefore Scripture says, \"in the first month, that is, the month Nisan,\" just as (Esther 3:7), \"he cast pur, that is, the lot.\" And to this day the nations in the lands of Persia and Media call them Nisan and Tishrei and all of them as we do.\n\nAnd now we shall mention in the months the second redemption, just as we have done until now in the first."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The final sentence 'And now we shall mention in the months the second redemption, just as we have done until now in the first' is difficult; it appears to refer to a liturgical or calendrical practice but is elliptical. Translated hyper-literally without expanding."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:22.825408397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}